* Competitive bidding

Booklet sections "DEFENSIVE BIDDING" and "COMPETITIVE BIDDING". Unless otherwise
noted, a bid or double by an opponent cancels a convention meant for
non-competitive sequences (for example 1!h-(2!s)-2NT reverts to natural,
12--14 HCP). A "direct" overcall/double follows the opening with no other calls
in between (`(1X)-`); a "balancing" one follows two passes, ours and then the
opener's partner's (`(1X)-P-(P)-`).

** Overcalls

A simple overcall shows 8--16 points and a 4+ card suit; the only forcing
response is a cuebid of the overcall suit, asking about quality. A 1NT overcall
shows 15--18 balanced with a stopper. A jump overcall is preemptive, to the same
values as the matching opening bid; a jump to 2NT is the unusual notrump,
5+5+ in the two lowest unbid suits. Since several of these calls' own hands
overlap (a two-suited hand also has 4+ in one of its suits, a preemptive
long-suit hand also has 4+ in that suit, and a takeout double's 12+ hcp also
covers every other call's range), the more descriptive, shape-bearing calls
are given a `{prio:N}` above both the plain one-level overcall and the double,
so a hand that qualifies for Michaels, the unusual notrump, the 1NT overcall or
a weak jump overcall is shown that way rather than folded into the plainer,
lower-priority `1Y`; the double is left as the catch-all for hands that do not
fit a shapelier call (`NOTES.md` #14 covers the unusual notrump's own
per-opening-suit shape).

(1X)-
1Y = {prio:2} 8--16 hcp, 4+#
D = 12+ hcp

(1m)-
2m = {prio:3} !Michaels, 8+ hcp, 5+!h, 5+!s

(1H)-
2H = {prio:3} !Michaels, 10+ hcp, 5+!s, 5+m

(1S)-
2S = {prio:3} !Michaels, 10+ hcp, 5+!h, 5+m

(1C)-
2N = {prio:3} !UNT, 5+!d, 5+!h

(1D)-
2N = {prio:3} !UNT, 5+!c, 5+!h

(1H)-
2N = {prio:3} !UNT, 5+!c, 5+!d

(1S)-
2N = {prio:3} !UNT, 5+!c, 5+!d

(1X)-1Y-
2X = F

*** Simple overcalls of a lower-ranking suit

`1Y` above only reaches suits ranked higher than the opening (`Y` binds above
`X`); a suit ranked lower costs an extra level to bid at all, so it needs a
slightly longer suit and cannot be as light as a same-level overcall. The
simple overcall of a lower suit is written out per opening suit at the two
level, to 10--16 hcp and a 5+ card suit.

(1D)-
2C = {prio:2} 10--16 hcp, 5+!c

(1H)-
2C = {prio:2} 10--16 hcp, 5+!c
2D = {prio:2} 10--16 hcp, 5+!d

(1S)-
2C = {prio:2} 10--16 hcp, 5+!c
2D = {prio:2} 10--16 hcp, 5+!d
2H = {prio:2} 10--16 hcp, 5+!h

*** Weak jump overcalls

A jump overcall at the two level is worth the same as a weak two in that suit; a
jump to the three level is worth the same as a three-level preempt. As with the
simple overcall above, `Y` only reaches suits ranked above the opening; a jump
overcall of a lower-ranking suit costs the same extra level as its simple
counterpart, so it is written out per opening suit at the three level (the
"jump" over a lower suit's own two-level simple overcall). The exact-length
jump shows the suit more precisely than the plain `1Y` overcall's own 4+, so it
is given a higher `{prio:3}` (above `1Y`'s `{prio:2}`): a 6-card suit within
the jump's 5--11/5--9 range is shown by jumping, not folded into the plain
one-level call.

(1X)-
2Y = {prio:3} 6=#, 5--11 hcp
3Y = {prio:3} 7=#, 5--9 hcp

(1D)-
3C = {prio:3} 6=!c, 5--11 hcp

(1H)-
3C = {prio:3} 6=!c, 5--11 hcp
3D = {prio:3} 6=!d, 5--11 hcp

(1S)-
3C = {prio:3} 6=!c, 5--11 hcp
3D = {prio:3} 6=!d, 5--11 hcp
3H = {prio:3} 6=!h, 5--11 hcp

*** Notrump overcalls

(1X)-
1N = {prio:3} 15--18 hcp, bal, stopper

There are no conventional responses opposite the 1NT overcall except 2!c, which
is Stayman; everything else is natural, as if partner had opened 1NT.

(1X)-1N-
2C = !STAY NF, 8+ hcp, 4+!h or 4+!s

** Takeout doubles

A double of an opening partscore bid (through 4!d) is for takeout; a double of an
opening game bid (4!h or higher) is for penalty. A below-game jump response is
invitational (5+ card suit); a plain new suit is minimum (4+ card suit, any
strength -- advancer must find a call); to force, responder cuebids the
opponent's suit. Both are written out per opening suit below (rather than with
the shared `Y`), because a shared `Y` binding cannot also state "and it is
*this* suit specifically" (each expansion needs its own `#!x` length atom, not
merely a distinct `{prio:N}`, or every unbid suit's row would compile to the
same unconstrained shape and accept a hand regardless of what it actually
holds -- see `NOTES.md` #20, this section's own former bug). A suit ranked
below the opening costs an extra level, exactly as for an overcall (`***
Simple overcalls of a lower-ranking suit` above): its minimum call is a
2-level (not 1-level) bid and its invitational jump a level above that. The
invitational jump in any suit outranks a plain minimum call in any other suit
(the same "more descriptive call wins" convention as `NOTES.md` #7): a hand
with invitational values and a 5+ card suit shows that directly rather than
settling for a cheap minimum bid in a different, shorter suit.

(1X)-D-
1N = 8--10 hcp
2X = F, 10+ hcp

(1C)-D-
2D = {prio:9} INV, 5+!d
2H = {prio:8} INV, 5+!h
2S = {prio:7} INV, 5+!s
1D = {prio:6} unlimited, 4+!d
1H = {prio:5} unlimited, 4+!h
1S = {prio:4} unlimited, 4+!s

(1D)-D-
2H = {prio:9} INV, 5+!h
2S = {prio:8} INV, 5+!s
3C = {prio:7} INV, 5+!c
1H = {prio:6} unlimited, 4+!h
1S = {prio:5} unlimited, 4+!s
2C = {prio:4} unlimited, 4+!c

(1H)-D-
2S = {prio:9} INV, 5+!s
3C = {prio:8} INV, 5+!c
3D = {prio:7} INV, 5+!d
1S = {prio:6} unlimited, 4+!s
2C = {prio:5} unlimited, 4+!c
2D = {prio:4} unlimited, 4+!d

(1S)-D-
3C = {prio:9} INV, 5+!c
3D = {prio:8} INV, 5+!d
3H = {prio:7} INV, 5+!h
2C = {prio:6} unlimited, 4+!c
2D = {prio:5} unlimited, 4+!d
2H = {prio:4} unlimited, 4+!h

** Negative doubles

Through 2!s, a double by responder after RHO's overcall promises 4+ cards (at
least) in an unbid major and 6+ points; a new suit bid at the two level or higher
instead shows 11+ points and a 5+ card suit. A same-level new suit that fits
under this ceiling (a 1-level suit still open after a 1-level overcall) is a
simple, non-forcing natural bid instead, exactly as it would be with no
interference; those rows are added here since the negative double competes
with them directly.

1C-(1D)-
D = 6+ hcp, 4+!s, 4+!h
1H = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!h
1S = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!s
2H = {prio:1} 11+ hcp, 5+!h
2S = {prio:1} 11+ hcp, 5+!s

1C-(1H)-
D = 6+ hcp, 4=!s
1S = F, 6+ hcp, 5+!s

1C-(1S)-
D = 4=!h and 6+ hcp or 5+!h and 5--10 hcp

1D-(1H)-
D = 6+ hcp, 4=!s
1S = F, 6+ hcp, 5+!s

1D-(1S)-
D = 4=!h and 6+ hcp or 5+!h and 5--10 hcp

** Responses to a double of our opening

If RHO doubles our opening, a new suit response is forcing regardless of point
count (but capped below redouble's own 10+ hcp, so the two do not compete for
the same hand); a two-level bid is non-forcing (6--10, usually a six-card suit,
written here with that suit so parallel two-level bids stay disjoint); 2NT is a
limit raise or better; redouble shows 10+ points (given priority over 2NT's
overlapping 10+ so a balanced maximum still has a way to show extra values); a
jump raise of our suit is preemptive with good support but fewer than 10 points;
a jump shift is to play, showing a six-plus card suit.

1D-(D)-
1H = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!h, not 10+ hcp
1S = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!s, not 10+ hcp
2C = NF, 6--10 hcp, 6+!c
2N = INV, 10+ hcp
R = {prio:1} 10+ hcp
3D = PRE, 6+!d
2H = 6+!h
2S = 6+!s
3C = 6+!c

1C-(D)-
1D = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!d, not 10+ hcp
1H = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!h, not 10+ hcp
1S = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!s, not 10+ hcp
2N = INV, 10+ hcp
R = {prio:1} 10+ hcp
3C = PRE, 6+!c

1H-(D)-
1S = F, 6+ hcp, 4+!s, not 10+ hcp
2C = NF, 6--10 hcp, 6+!c
2D = NF, 6--10 hcp, 6+!d
2N = INV, 10+ hcp
R = {prio:1} 10+ hcp
3H = PRE, 6+!h

1S-(D)-
2C = NF, 6--10 hcp, 6+!c
2D = NF, 6--10 hcp, 6+!d
2H = NF, 6--10 hcp, 6+!h
2N = INV, 10+ hcp
R = {prio:1} 10+ hcp
3S = PRE, 6+!s

** Responses to an overcall and to Michaels/the unusual notrump

A cuebid of opener's suit is the only forcing response to a simple overcall, and
asks about the quality of the overcall. Over a major-suit Michaels cuebid, 2NT
asks for the minor. If the opponents use a two-suited convention (Michaels or
the unusual notrump) against our own opening, a double shows 10+ points
(probably balanced), and cuebidding one of their known suits is a game force;
which suit that is depends on what their cuebid showed, so it is spelled out per
opening suit below rather than with a variable (see NOTES.md #14).

(1X)-1Y-(P)-
2X = F

(1H)-2H-(P)-
2N = F

(1S)-2S-(P)-
2N = F

1H-(2H)-
D = 10+ hcp
3S = F

1S-(2S)-
D = 10+ hcp
3H = F

1C-(2C)-
D = 10+ hcp
3H = F, 4+!h
3S = F, 4+!s

1D-(2D)-
D = 10+ hcp
3H = F, 4+!h
3S = F, 4+!s

1X-(2N)-
D = 10+ hcp

** Balancing

A reopening bid means much the same as a direct-seat bid, but it can be lighter,
especially the takeout double and the 1NT overcall. The plain suit overcall
outranks the double (review finding: with no exclusion the double, appearing
first, otherwise won every tied-priority hand that also held a real 4-card
suit, so a hand worth a genuine reopening overcall was shown as a takeout
double instead); the jump overcall is given a distinct, lower-strength band
(6--10, not 1Y's own 6--16) so it is a genuine preemptive jump with extra
length, not merely "the same hand, one card longer" (review finding: sharing
1Y's full range meant *any* 5+ card hand jumped, including strong ones that
should overcall calmly at the one level instead).

(1X)-P-(P)-
1Y = {prio:1} 6--16 hcp, 4+#
D = {prio:-1} 8+ hcp
1N = {prio:1} 10--15 hcp, bal, stopper
2Y = {prio:2} 6--10 hcp, 5+#
